! mcp_bootstrap_p7_p8.strap -- MCP's IPL BOOTSTRAP, printed pages 7
! and 8: X ERR DD, the error table, and the definitions that go with
! it.  docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF pp.14-15, headers "PAGE NUMBER
! 7" and "PAGE NUMBER 8".  docs/boot_plan.md M3.
!
! THE LAST TWO PAGES OF THE BOOTSTRAP TO BE TRANSCRIBED, and they are
! the index to everything else: thirty-nine words, one per error code,
! each a pair of VF half words holding the address of the site that
! failed and the address of the message to print for it.  printed p.5
! line 8's "X ERR SH  KV,$1,0.0($2)" is the loop that scans it.
!
! THE TABLE CHECKS ITS OWN LENGTH.  Printed p.8 line 31 defines
! "X E DD K  SYN,$-X ERR DD  -SIZE OF ERROR TABLE" and the listing
! prints its value as 000047.00+ -- thirty-nine words, which is
! exactly what the seventy-eight VF half words below occupy, and
! exactly the number of distinct error codes (00 through 29, plus
! 05A-05E, 16A-16C and 21A).  Nothing had to be assumed about where
! the table ends.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THREE THINGS THE TABLE SAYS ABOUT MCP
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) EIGHT ENTRIES SHARE FIVE MESSAGES.  000432.00 appears four
!     times -- X MSG 15, X MSG 16, X MS 16A and X MS 16B -- and
!     000464.00 and 000501.00 twice each, although the listing writes
!     a different descriptive comment beside each.  The message block
!     on printed pp.9-13 is otherwise strictly sequential, so three
!     of the described messages have no storage of their own: several
!     distinct failures deliberately print the same text.
!
! (2) THE TABLE IS NOT IN ERROR-CODE ORDER.  The code-10 pair sits
!     between 05E and 06, and everything else is sequential.  That
!     rules out indexing by code and fits X ERR IN being a SEARCH
!     word -- "XW,X ERR DD,X E DD K+1.0,0" -- scanned by p.5's KV
!     loop rather than subscripted.
!
! (3) X ERR 20 AND X ERR 21 ARE NOT "SIC,X ERR AD" ROWS.  Every other
!     error site stores its instruction counter into X ERR AD so the
!     routine can print where it failed; these two are "BD,$",
!     branch-disabled-to-self, and simply halt.  Their messages are
!     "NO CONSOLE DEFINED" and "NO CONSOLE AVAILABLE" -- with no
!     console there is nothing to print to, so there is nothing to
!     store an address for.  The two corroborate each other, and
!     their printed binaries are each their own location plus .04.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! TWO SUBSTITUTIONS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (a) RETIRED 2026-10-22 -- LINE 34 IS NOW VERBATIM.  It had been
!     written "CW(CCR),X ZEROS,3,X MSG CW" in place of the page's
!     "CW,X ZEROS,3,X MSG CW(.25)1", with this note:
!
!       "The '(.25)1' is a bit designation -- set bit 25 of the
!        generated word to one... This assembler does not implement
!        bit designations, and inventing their general semantics from
!        one row is what AGENTS.md rule 4 forbids."
!
!     That was right, and it held for four months.  The construct is
!     STRAP's PARENTHETICAL INTEGER ENTRY MODE and it is specified in
!     full in C28-6129 Section II, printed pp.25-26 (PDF 29-30),
!     under the heading "Field Entry Mode" -- see
!     decks/parenthetical_entry.strap, which quotes it.  The
!     assembler implements it now, so the page's own spelling is what
!     this deck carries.
!
!     THE SUBSTITUTION'S OWN ARGUMENT IS NOW A TEST.  C28-6129
!     printed p.14's CCR, "Chain Counts Within Record", is chain 1
!     multiple 0 skip 0 -- the same three bits "(.25)1" sets -- so
!     the two spellings must assemble to the identical word.  They
!     do, and verify_parenthetical_entry.py checks exactly that
!     equality rather than taking it on trust.
!
! (b) LINE 35 IS "CW,0,10,0", NOT "CW,0,10,C".  The scan renders a
!     leading digit zero with a gap on the right, so it reads as a C
!     (docs/listing_typography.md 13).  The arithmetic settles it: the
!     row's refill field prints 000000.
!
! A BARE "CW" WITH NO SECONDARY, as lines 35 and 37 write, means all
! three flags zero -- the same bits CR sets -- and both rows print
! 000 in the flag group.  strap_asm used to demand one of the eight
! secondaries, which would have made these two rows unassemblable.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS.  EVERY SYN IS AN INPUT, NOT A WITNESS, so each is taken
! from the page that DEFINES it and none from this page's own values.
! The thirty-nine error sites come from printed pp.3, 4 and 15-32 --
! sixteen of them already transcribed in this repository, the rest
! read off their defining rows, all but two "SIC,X ERR AD" printing
! 000551.00 80.  The thirty-nine message labels are taken from
! programs/mcp_bootstrap_p9_p12.strap and _p13.strap, which place
! them from the instruction stream; the verifier asserts that those
! two programs and these SYNs agree, so the two transcriptions cannot
! silently diverge.
! ---- THE ERROR SITES, each from the page that defines it ----
! All but two are "SIC,X ERR AD" rows printing 000551.00 80.
! X ERR 20 and X ERR 21 are "BD,$" -- see the header.
XER05A  SYN, (8)000071.00
XER05B  SYN, (8)000072.00
XER05C  SYN, (8)000073.00
XER05D  SYN, (8)000075.00
